  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🏠 Roofing Company Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for roofers, from Slate Expectations to Storm Ridge Roofing."
    },
    "intro": "Good roofing company names sound dependable in any weather: a family name with <em>Roofing</em>, a strong word like <em>Ridge</em> or <em>Storm</em>, or a pun on slates and gutters. Starting a roofing firm? This **roofing company name generator** has catchy and funny puns, classic family names, strong storm-proof names and short modern ones, plus names from your town or your first name, with a slogan if you want one.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good roofing company names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator:</p><ul><li><strong>Fully Covered</strong>: a catchy name that's also a promise.</li><li><strong>Hip &amp; Valley Roofers</strong>: two parts of a roof, so it sounds like you know your trade.</li><li><strong>Gutterly Brilliant</strong>: a pun people remember, ideal for gutter cleaning too.</li><li><strong>A Ridge Too Far</strong>: a film pun on the ridge, the top line of a roof.</li><li><strong>Sawyer Roofing &amp; Leadwork</strong>: a classic family name that names a specialist skill.</li><li><strong>Ridgeline Roofing &amp; Restoration</strong>: strong and professional, for bigger jobs.</li><li><strong>Truss Roofing Co.</strong>: short and modern, named after the timber frame under the tiles.</li><li><strong>Hampton Slaters</strong>: a town name and a trade name. Put your own town in the box.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What makes a roofing name work",
        "content": "<p>Roofing has its own vocabulary, and customers trust a name that uses it properly:</p><ul><li><strong>Roof words:</strong> a <em>ridge</em> is the top line of a roof, a <em>hip</em> the sloping edge where two slopes meet, a <em>valley</em> the channel between them and a <em>gable</em> the triangle of wall at the end. A <em>truss</em> is the frame that holds it all up.</li><li><strong>Specialisms:</strong> slaters, thatchers, leadwork, flat roofs, guttering, and fascias and soffits are all searched for. Naming one tells people you do it.</li><li><strong>Strong names</strong> with <em>Storm</em>, <em>Shield</em> or <em>Fortress</em> suit a firm that does repairs after bad weather.</li><li><strong>Puns</strong> work well on vans and ladders: <em>Slate Expectations</em>, <em>Shingle Minded</em>.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Check it's free before you print the sign",
        "content": "<p>A roofing name ends up on a van, a website, a Google Business Profile and a stack of invoices, so it's worth a few minutes' checking before any of that is printed:</p><ul><li><strong>Press Check</strong> on any name above. The <a href=\"../business-name-checker.html\">Business Name Checker</a> looks up the domain, links to the company register (Companies House in the UK) and opens the trade mark search and social handles.</li><li><strong>Search your area.</strong> Look up the name on a map and in local search results. Two firms with the same name a few miles apart confuse customers and reviews.</li><li><strong>Avoid names close to a well-known roofing brand or product, which can cause trade mark problems.</strong></li></ul><p>This is general guidance, not legal advice. For the full process, from shortlisting to registering, read our guide to <a href=\"../guides/business-naming-best-practices.html\">Business Naming Best Practices</a>.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Making the name work for you",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Add a slogan</strong> that promises dry homes: choose <em>Name with a slogan</em> for lines like <em>Leaks fixed, homes protected</em>.</li><li><strong>Put the town in</strong> if most of your work is local. It helps people find you after a storm.</li><li><strong>Use the same name</strong> on the van, the ladders' signboards, invoices and your Google Business Profile.</li></ul><p>Other trades: the <a href=\"plumbing-business-name-generator.html\">Plumbing</a>, <a href=\"electrical-business-name-generator.html\">Electrical</a>, <a href=\"painting-business-name-generator.html\">Painting</a>, <a href=\"construction-company-name-generator.html\">Construction</a>, <a href=\"cleaning-business-name-generator.html\">Cleaning</a>, <a href=\"landscaping-business-name-generator.html\">Landscaping</a> business name generators. For any kind of firm, try the <a href=\"business-name-generator.html\">Business Name Generator</a>.</p><p>For more, see all the <a href=\"../categories/biz_trades-names.html\">trades and home services business name generators</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What's a good name for a roofing company?",
        "answer": "One that's easy to read on a van and sounds dependable: your surname with <em>Roofing</em> (<em>Hartley Roofing &amp; Guttering</em>), a strong weather word (<em>Storm Ridge Roofing</em>) or a friendly pun (<em>In Truss We Trust</em>). Check the name is free locally before you print anything."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do I check a roofing company name is free?",
        "answer": "Press <strong>Check</strong> next to any name to see if it's free as a company, a domain and a social media handle. Then make sure: in the UK, search Companies House and the UK IPO trade mark register; in the US, search your state's business register and the USPTO trade mark search. A quick map search for the name in your area helps too."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should I put my town in a roofing company name?",
        "answer": "If most of your work is local, yes: a name like Hampton Slaters helps people find you when they search after a storm. If you plan to cover a wide area, a strong name like Ridgeline Roofing &amp; Restoration travels better."
      }
    ]
